# Tooltip Input Polling Implementation Plan

## Status

Phases 0 through 4 are complete. Phase 5 is the next implementation step. This
document is the execution contract for pointer sampling, registered-control
hit testing, and tooltip-service notification.

Tooltip rendering is intentionally excluded. A separate plan will decide how
tooltip presentation is placed above base-game and DFHack content without
participating in focus, input, or logic.

## Goal

Replace tooltip hover detection that depends on a full-screen `gui.ZScreen`
render or input lifecycle with a process-wide polling path that:

- samples the current pointer independently of UI input consumption,
- resolves at most one registered tooltip target for each sample,
- preserves DwarfUI pointer enter, leave, and update behavior,
- publishes a presentation-neutral tooltip intent to the tooltip service, and
- creates no screen, overlay, widget, focus owner, input handler, or logic
  participant.

The polling and detection code must not know how, where, or whether a tooltip
is eventually rendered.

## Scope boundary

This plan owns:

- the recurring pointer-sampling lifecycle,
- one `dfhack.screen.getMousePos()` read per polling tick,
- registered-control hit testing,
- target arbitration across eligible registered roots,
- pointer enter, leave, and update transitions,
- tooltip-text validation and snapshotting,
- publication and clearing of the tooltip service's current intent,
- reload-safe cancellation,
- unit, package, and live automation evidence for those behaviors.

This plan does not own:

- `gui.ZScreen`, `OverlayWidget`, or any other presentation host,
- tooltip drawing, painter selection, z-order, clipping, sizing, wrapping, or
  screen-edge placement,
- renderer activation, invalidation, or refresh cadence,
- focus strings, `hasFocus()`, `getCurViewscreen()`, `isActive()`, or `raise()`,
- forwarding or consuming mouse, keyboard, or game input,
- choosing the final rendering integration.

The existing renderer may remain temporarily available to tests and production
code, but the polling implementation must not import it or call it. Production
cutover is deferred until the rendering plan supplies a consumer for the
published intent.

## Confirmed architecture

### Layer 1: process-wide pointer poller

Add a generic `PointerPoller` that owns only scheduling and pointer
acquisition. It must not import tooltip, overlay, or GUI presentation modules.

For each tick it publishes exactly one immutable sample:

```lua
---@class dwarfui.PointerSample
---@field sequence integer
---@field x integer|nil
---@field y integer|nil
---@field coordinate_space '"screen-cells"'
```

Both coordinates are normalized to `nil` when the pointer is unavailable.
`sequence` is monotonically increasing within the current service generation.

The poller accepts an observer callback. It does not know that its observer is
the tooltip system and does not retain any widget, root, renderer, or
viewscreen.

### Layer 2: tooltip target detector

Add a tooltip-specific detector that consumes `PointerSample` values and the
service's weak registration set. It uses the existing
`PointerDispatcher.resolve()` contract to produce exactly one observation:

```lua
---@class dwarfui.TooltipPointerObservation
---@field sequence integer
---@field kind '"target"'|'"blocked"'|'"miss"'
---@field pointer_x integer|nil
---@field pointer_y integer|nil
---@field target gui.View|nil
---@field local_x integer|nil
---@field local_y integer|nil
---@field root gui.View|nil
```

The detector owns root eligibility and cross-root arbitration. It does not run
pointer callbacks, read tooltip text, retain the current target, or call a
presentation object.

A pointer outside all eligible registered roots, an unavailable pointer, or a
pointer blocked before reaching a registered target produces a non-target
observation. The distinction between `blocked` and `miss` remains observable
for diagnostics and focused tests, but neither can select a tooltip target.

### Layer 3: tooltip service mediation

The tooltip service accepts observations and owns semantic tooltip state:

1. Compare the observed target with the prior target.
2. Deliver `on_pointer_leave` and `on_pointer_enter` exactly once per target
   transition.
3. Deliver `on_pointer_update` for every target observation.
4. Read and validate `target.tooltip` after callbacks have run so dynamic
   tooltip text reflects the current target-local coordinates.
5. Publish or clear one immutable `TooltipIntent`.

```lua
---@class dwarfui.TooltipIntent
---@field revision integer
---@field source_sequence integer
---@field text string
---@field anchor_x integer
---@field anchor_y integer
---@field coordinate_space '"screen-cells"'
---@field source_root gui.View
```

The intent contains a text snapshot, a screen-cell anchor, and the opaque root
that supplied the target. The input system does not classify or interpret the
root for presentation; it merely preserves the identity already produced by
hit testing so a presentation consumer can reject surfaces it cannot render
above. The intent does not contain a renderer frame, dimensions, wrapping
result, screen bounds, painter, overlay name, focus string, or viewscreen.

The service exposes the current intent through a presentation-neutral
subscription or observer boundary. Selection between those two equivalent
forms belongs to implementation, but the contract must provide:

- immediate notification when a new intent replaces the prior intent,
- immediate notification when the intent is cleared,
- a monotonically increasing revision so consumers can reject stale work, and
- no requirement that a consumer be present.

The input path ends at this service boundary. The rendering plan will define a
consumer without changing the polling, detection, or observation contracts.

## Lifecycle and ownership decisions

- The process-wide state remains in `dfhack.dwarfui` so same-version module
  reloads can identify and retire older scheduled callbacks.
- Registrations remain weak-keyed and must not keep controls alive.
- The first live registration starts polling; removal or collection of the
  final registration stops rescheduling.
- At most one polling callback may be scheduled for the active generation.
- Each start or reload establishes a new generation token. A callback whose
  captured token is stale exits without sampling, notifying, or rescheduling.
- Explicit shutdown increments the generation before clearing state so a
  callback already queued by DFHack cannot resurrect the old service.
- A tick re-counts live weak registrations before sampling and before
  rescheduling. Collection of the final registration therefore converges to a
  stopped poller without an explicit `unregister()`.
- An observer error must not create duplicate callback chains. The error is
  surfaced with context, and the service remains in a state where a deliberate
  restart or reload can establish one new chain.
- Polling is registration-gated, not map-load-gated. Since it creates no
  viewscreen and performs no UI-stack operation, it can remain structurally
  safe during title, world-selection, loading, and map transitions. Live
  characterization must confirm the chosen DFHack scheduler behaves as
  expected in those states.
- The production cadence starts at one frame per sample. The scheduler and
  sampler remain injectable in unit tests, and cadence can be made
  configurable later without changing detector or service contracts.

## Target eligibility and arbitration decisions

- A registered widget is eligible only when it and every attached ancestor are
  visible and active.
- A detached widget remains registered but cannot win hit testing.
- A root must have current layout bounds before it can be tested.
- Registered `OverlayWidget` roots must still be the registry-owned enabled
  instance and must declare the current native viewscreen.
- Non-overlay roots require a presentation-state predicate that can be tested
  without referring to the future tooltip renderer. Existing native-mounted
  component behavior must be characterized before this predicate is changed.
- Tooltip infrastructure itself is never a candidate root.
- Each eligible root is resolved once through
  `PointerDispatcher.resolve(root, x, y)`.
- Only a resolved target that is itself currently registered can win. A
  registered descendant cannot be inferred from an unrelated ancestor hit.
- Reverse subview order remains the within-root z-order contract.
- The currently established registration-sequence rule remains the
  cross-root tie breaker until a supported, presentation-independent root
  ordering source is proven. The detector must isolate this policy behind one
  documented arbitration function so it can be replaced without changing
  sampling or service mediation.
- One sample yields no more than one winning target and one service
  observation.

## Phase 0: Characterize scheduler and current behavior

- [x] Record the current production registration, hit-testing, target
      transition, dynamic-text, and teardown behavior in focused tests before
      refactoring it.
- [x] Add a minimal live probe proving whether a recurring
      `dfhack.timeout(1, 'frames', callback)` continues to run while the game is
      paused and while a foreground widget consumes mouse input.
- [x] Prove that reading `dfhack.screen.getMousePos()` from the timeout callback
      neither consumes input nor changes the current viewscreen or focus
      string.
- [x] Characterize timeout behavior at the title screen, during a safe
      load-state transition, and after map load without leaving a recurring
      callback behind.
- [x] Confirm how native-mounted DwarfSpec roots can be recognized as
      currently presented without consulting a tooltip presentation object.
- [x] Record the current cross-root arbitration result for overlapping
      eligible roots.
- [x] Treat any scheduler behavior that depends on a screen's render,
      `onInput`, or `onIdle` method as a failed feasibility result and stop
      before production refactoring.

### Phase 0 completion gate

- [x] The recurring scheduler has live evidence that it observes pointer
      movement independently of UI input dispatch.
- [x] Pointer sampling has no observed focus, viewscreen, pause, or input
      side effect.
- [x] Timeout teardown and reload behavior are understood well enough to write
      deterministic lifecycle tests.
- [x] Root eligibility inputs are identified without referencing the tooltip
      renderer.

### Phase 0 evidence

- Current behavior is locked by
  `tests/tooltip_registration.spec.lua` and `tests/pointer.spec.lua`.
  The focused run reported `29 successes / 0 failures / 0 errors`. It covers
  idempotent registration, reverse-subview hit testing, blocked and missed
  targets, enter/update/leave ordering, dynamic text, attachment and
  visibility changes, explicit removal, weak collection, reload, and
  registration-sequence priority between overlapping roots.
- `tests/tooltip/tooltip_pointer_scheduler.ds.lua` is the minimal live probe.
  Its focused DwarfSpec run reported `2 successes / 0 failures / 0 errors`
  with `cleanup_confirmed=true` and `mount_cleanup_verified=true`. The
  recurring callback observed pointer movement while paused, continued after
  its foreground screen consumed left-click input, preserved the focus list
  returned by DwarfSpec's `getFocusList()` command, preserved
  `dfhack.gui.getCurViewscreen(true)` identity and pause state, and stopped
  rescheduling after its demand flag was cleared.
- The installed DFHack 53.15 scheduler implementation establishes the
  title/load/map contract. `library/LuaTools.cpp` stores frame timers in
  `frame_timers`, advances them from `Lua::Core::onUpdate()`, and cancels only
  `tick_timers` on map or world unload. `library/Core.cpp` calls that update
  from the process-wide core update path without consulting a viewscreen
  render, input, or idle method. A frame timer therefore remains queued across
  title and load-state transitions and resumes on core updates; it does not
  execute while a synchronous load blocks the core update loop.
- A currently presented DwarfSpec native root is identified without a
  presentation object by identity with
  `dfhack.gui.getDFViewscreen(true).widgets`. The live probe confirms that
  `ds.mountNativeScreen()` borrows this exact root. Registered overlays retain
  their existing registry-owned enabled-instance and viewscreen-match inputs.
- Teardown uses an explicit demand flag plus a generation token in the
  planned implementation. The live probe proves a queued callback becomes
  inert and drains without rescheduling; the current focused reload coverage
  proves the registration set and target behavior that the generation handoff
  must preserve.
- Lua syntax validation passed for all 50 production, test, and support Lua
  files; unit-test naming and `git diff --check` passed. The complete unit run
  reached `183 successes / 0 failures / 1 error`: the error is the pre-existing
  modified special-screen workaround indexing a missing package-test `df`
  global from `tooltip_registration.lua`. Phase 0 does not alter that
  superseded workaround, and the failure is not counted as passing evidence.

